<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Zambia</h1><p class="st-meta">1964 CE – present · capital Lusaka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked country in Southern Africa, independent from Britain in 1964, a one-party state from 1972 to 1991 and a multiparty state since.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> became the Republic of Zambia on 24 October 1964, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> was dissolved on 31 December 1963. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a>, who had won the only election for Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> in January 1964, became the first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kaunda's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party">United National Independence Party</a> (UNIP) held power from 1964 to 1991, and from 1972 Zambia was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> under the motto &quot;One Zambia, One Nation&quot;. Kaunda backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> fighting in Southern Rhodesia, which led to the border's closure in 1973. A fall in the price of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, the principal export, in the mid-1970s led to a debt crisis. Riots in June 1990 killed many protesters, and Kaunda agreed in 1991 to restore multiparty democracy and lost the election to Frederick Chiluba.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Zambia has seen peaceful transitions of power since 1991. Michael Sata was president from 2011 until his death on 28 October 2014, and Edgar Lungu won the election in January 2015. In the 2021 general elections Hakainde Hichilema won 59% of the vote, and Lungu conceded. Hichilema was sworn in on 24 August 2021. The 1996 constitution declares Zambia a Christian nation while protecting freedom of religion.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zambia.jpg" alt="Zambia" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Gabriel Ellison · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Zambia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1964 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lusaka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kenneth Kaunda, Frederick Chiluba, Michael Sata, Edgar Lungu, Hakainde Hichilema</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Protestant and Catholic)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Bemba, Nyanja</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Zambia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1995&amp;focus=zambia" aria-label="Zambia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Zambia lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4858 3767L4793 3693M4858 3767L4426 3768"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4793 3693h0M4426 3768h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4858 3767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4858 3767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lusaka. <a href="/atlas?year=1995&amp;focus=zambia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
